                <pb facs="0313" n="193" sameAs="frus:frus1931v02/pg193"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">816.01/11: Telegram</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">The Secretary of State to
                        the Chargé in Honduras</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic"
                            >Higgins</hi>)</head>
                <opener><dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Washington</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1931-12-08T18:00:00-05:00">December 8, 1931—6
                        p.m.</date></dateline></opener>
                <p>90. Your 188, December 7, noon, first paragraph, and 190, December 8, 11 a.m. You
                    may assure the Minister for Foreign Affairs that as soon as the Department is in
                    a position to determine the course it will follow it will communicate with the
                    Honduran Government and with the other Central American Governments concerning
                    the matter. The Department is giving the most careful consideration to all
                    aspects of the situation but cannot determine its course of action until the
                    receipt of further reports which it is now awaiting from Salvador.</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">Stimson</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
